.Dd September 16, 2026
.Dt NDI_DEVCTL_IOCTL 9F
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ndi_devctl_ioctl ,
.Nm ndi_devctl_device_getstate ,
.Nm ndi_devctl_device_online ,
.Nm ndi_devctl_device_offline ,
.Nm ndi_devctl_device_remove ,
.Nm ndi_devctl_bus_getstate
.Nd implement common device-control operations for a nexus
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/sunndi.h
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_devctl_ioctl
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fa "int cmd"
.Fa "intptr_t arg"
.Fa "int mode"
.Fa "uint_t flags"
.Fc
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_devctl_device_getstate
.Fa "dev_info_t *parent"
.Fa "struct devctl_iocdata *dcp"
.Fa "uint_t *state"
.Fc
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_devctl_device_online
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fa "struct devctl_iocdata *dcp"
.Fa "uint_t flags"
.Fc
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_devctl_device_offline
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fa "struct devctl_iocdata *dcp"
.Fa "uint_t flags"
.Fc
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_devctl_device_remove
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fa "struct devctl_iocdata *dcp"
.Fa "uint_t flags"
.Fc
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_devctl_bus_getstate
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fa "struct devctl_iocdata *dcp"
.Fa "uint_t *state"
.Fc
.Sh INTERFACE LEVEL
illumos specific
.Pq Consolidation Private .
.Sh PARAMETERS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va dip , parent
Attached parent nexus; device operations address its children.
.It Va cmd
Device-control ioctl command.
.It Va arg
User address of the request.
.It Va mode
Ioctl mode argument; currently unused.
.It Va flags
NDI configuration flags forwarded to the operation.
.It Va dcp
Request returned by \fBndi_dc_allochdl()\fP.
.It Va state
Kernel address receiving a state value.
.El
.Sh DESCRIPTION
\fBndi_devctl_ioctl()\fP copies in a user device-control request, executes the
operation, copies out a state result when required, and frees the request.
It handles \fBDEVCTL_BUS_GETSTATE\fP, \fBDEVCTL_DEVICE_GETSTATE\fP,
\fBDEVCTL_DEVICE_ONLINE\fP, \fBDEVCTL_DEVICE_OFFLINE\fP, \fBDEVCTL_DEVICE_REMOVE\fP,
and \fBDEVCTL_BUS_DEV_CREATE\fP.
Reset, attachment-point, and bus quiesce operations require bus-specific
implementations and return \fBENOTSUP\fP.
Unrecognized commands return \fBENOTTY\fP after request copyin.
.Pp
The \fBmode\fP argument is currently unused.
Copying uses the current process's user data model and user address space,
even if \fBFKIOCTL\fP is present in \fBmode\fP.
The caller must perform the access and privilege checks appropriate to the
nexus's control device before invoking this helper.
.Pp
The other functions operate on an already copied \fBdcp\fP.
The device functions identify an immediate child using the request's node
name and unit address.
The online function configures that child and its descendants.
The offline function detaches it and marks it offline.
The remove function unconfigures it and removes its node.
Offline and remove clean cached devfs references before unconfiguration;
they must not be called while holding conflicting devfs lookup locks.
.Pp
\fBndi_devctl_device_getstate()\fP stores \fBDEVICE_OFFLINE\fP, \fBDEVICE_DOWN\fP, or
\fBDEVICE_ONLINE\fP in \fB*state\fP, adding \fBDEVICE_BUSY\fP when an online device is
referenced.
\fBndi_devctl_bus_getstate()\fP stores the parent nexus's recorded bus state.
These two helpers write kernel memory, without copying a result to userland.
.Sh CONTEXT
\fBndi_devctl_ioctl()\fP must be called from user context.
The helpers using a copied request may be called from user or kernel context.
These operations may sleep.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The getstate helpers return \fBNDI_SUCCESS\fP or \fBNDI_FAILURE\fP.
The online, offline, and remove helpers return zero on success, \fBEINVAL\fP
when a name or address is absent, \fBEBUSY\fP for \fBNDI_BUSY\fP, and \fBEIO\fP for
\fBNDI_FAILURE\fP.
Other statuses from the underlying configuration operation pass through.
.Pp
\fBndi_devctl_ioctl()\fP returns the selected helper status without generally
translating NDI codes to errno values.
Initial request-copy failure returns \fBEFAULT\fP, but a state-result copyout
failure returns \fBNDI_FAULT\fP.
Unsupported and unknown commands return \fBENOTSUP\fP and \fBENOTTY\fP, respectively.
Callers must account for these mixed conventions before returning an error
from an ioctl entry point.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ioctl 9E ,
.Xr devfs_clean 9F ,
.Xr ndi_dc_allochdl 9F ,
.Xr ndi_dc_devi_create 9F ,
.Xr ndi_devi_config 9F ,
.Xr ndi_get_bus_state 9F ,
.Xr sunndi 9F
